Article 5 (Organizational Analysis and Diagnosis)
(1) The Minister of Education shall conduct an organizational analysis of local educational administrative agencies every year pursuant to Article 23 (1) of the Decree and determine, based on the results, whether to conduct an organizational diagnosis. <Amended on March 23, 2013.>
(2) In conducting an organizational analysis or diagnosis under paragraph (1), the Minister of Education shall consider regional conditions, including the scale of local educational administrative agencies, demand for educational administration and financial capacity. <Amended on March 23, 2013.>
(3) In conducting an organizational analysis or diagnosis under paragraph (1), the Minister of Education shall prepare a detailed plan in advance and notify the relevant local educational administrative agency thereof. <Amended on March 23, 2013.>
(4) The Minister of Education shall focus on the following fields when conducting an organizational analysis or diagnosis: <Amended on March 23, 2013.>
1. Fields requiring adjustment of organizational functions toward field support for schools and users of educational services;
2. Fields in which organizational functions are similar or overlapping or are expected to decline;
3. Fields in which agencies directly under the relevant organizations, etc. may be consolidated or abolished;
4. Fields of work that may be entrusted to the private sector;
5. Fields requiring adjustment of functions among organizations;
6. Organizations in which the proportion of personnel expenses in the budget is excessively high.
(5) Where the Minister of Education discloses the results of an organizational analysis or diagnosis under Article 23 (2) of the Decree, the results shall be published in the Official Gazette or a daily newspaper or posted on an Internet website. <Amended on March 23, 2013.>
(6) An organizational management improvement plan under Article 23 (3) of the Decree shall include the following matters:
1. Directions for improving organizational management identified from the results of organizational analysis and diagnosis;
2. Matters concerning organizational restructuring and personnel adjustment reflecting those results;
3. Matters that could not reflect the results of organizational analysis and diagnosis, and the reasons and grounds therefor;
4. Measures for utilizing and resolving surplus personnel arising from organizational restructuring and personnel adjustment;
5. Other matters necessary for efficient operation of organizations and personnel.
